# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in your Firebase web app config.
# Find these values in Firebase Console -> Project settings -> Your apps -> Web app -> SDK setup and configuration.
# All NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are safe to expose in the browser (they are not secrets),
# but keep .env.local out of git (it already is via .gitignore).

NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# Leave the six values above blank until you have set them. When they are
# empty the app runs in local mode (data saved in this browser only, no
# sign-in) so you can develop offline.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Drive storage
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Budget data is NOT stored in Firestore. Once a user signs in with Google,
# their settings, transactions and reports live in a hidden, app-only folder
# in their OWN Google Drive (Drive's "appDataFolder") — invisible in their
# normal Drive UI, readable only by this app, and free to read/write against
# (unlike Firestore, which bills per document read and write). Firebase is
# only used for sign-in and one small profile document per user.
#
# This needs its own one-time setup in Google Cloud Console, once you have
# created the Firebase project above:
#
# 1. Enable the Drive API
#    Console: https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/library/drive.googleapis.com
#    Pick the SAME Google Cloud project your Firebase project uses, then
#    click "Enable".
#
# 2. Find the OAuth client id Firebase already created for Google sign-in
#    Console: https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
#    Look for "Web client (auto created by Google Service)" — copy its
#    Client ID into NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID below. Reusing this exact
#    client id (rather than creating a new one) is what lets the app renew
#    Drive access quietly in the background instead of asking the user to
#    sign in again every hour.
#
# 3. Add the Drive scope to the OAuth consent screen
#    Console: https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials/consent
#    Under "Data access" / "Scopes", add:
#      https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.appdata
#    This scope only ever grants access to the app's own hidden folder —
#    never to the rest of a user's Drive.
#
# 4. Add your domain(s) to "Authorized JavaScript origins" on that same Web
#    client (e.g. http://localhost:3000 for development, plus your deployed
#    URL), so both Firebase's sign-in popup and the silent-refresh library
#    are allowed to run there.
#
# Note: while this app is in Google's "Testing" publishing status, only the
# test users you list in the consent screen can sign in — that's fine for
# development. Moving to "In production" with the drive.appdata scope
# requires Google's standard OAuth verification review before the public can
# use it (a privacy policy URL and a short verification form; appdata is a
# "sensitive", not "restricted", scope, so no security audit is required).
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
